# XSH lfind() and lsearch()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Both are XSI. The comparison functions in these tests return 0 for equal
# and non-zero otherwise, with no ordering, as the page allows; the tables
# hold distinct entries, so which of several matches is returned never
# matters.

assertion  XSH.lfind.001
anchor     functions/lfind.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/lfind.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       lfind() of an entry present in the table returns a pointer to that
           element (inside the table) and leaves the count unchanged.
test       t/001-lfind-found.c

assertion  XSH.lfind.002
anchor     functions/lfind.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/lfind.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       lfind() of an entry that is not present returns a null pointer and
           changes neither the table nor the count; on an empty table it
           returns a null pointer.
test       t/002-lfind-missing.c

assertion  XSH.lfind.003
anchor     functions/lfind.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/lfind.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       lsearch() of an entry already in the table returns a pointer to that
           element and neither adds an entry nor changes the count.
test       t/003-lsearch-found.c

assertion  XSH.lfind.004
anchor     functions/lfind.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/lfind.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       lsearch() of an entry that is not present copies it to the end of
           the table (the position after the last of the nel entries),
           increments the count by one, and returns a pointer to the new
           element; the entry is then found by lfind(), earlier entries are
           unchanged, and a second lsearch() of it adds nothing.
test       t/004-lsearch-append.c

assertion  XSH.lfind.005
anchor     functions/lfind.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       With 8-byte string records, a comparison function that returns
           only 0 or 1, and an empty table, lsearch() appends at the table's
           start, later strings after it, and lfind() finds each by content
           (a key in a different buffer).
test       t/005-records.c
